+pysemver sort
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Sort versions, oldest first.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver sort [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to sort. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+The sorted versions are printed on *standard out* as plain text,
+one version per line, so the output can be read with a ``while read``
+loop::
+
+    $ pysemver sort 1.10.0 1.2.0 1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0-rc.1
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+    $ printf '1.10.0\n1.2.0\n' | pysemver sort
+    1.2.0
+    1.10.0
+
+If any argument (or input line) is not a valid version, nothing is printed on standard out
+and the command exits with return code 2.
+
+
+pysemver latest
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Print the highest version that is not a prerelease.
+
+.. code:: bash
+
+   pysemver latest [<VERSION>...]
+
+.. option:: <VERSION>
+
+    Versions to choose from. If none are given, versions are read from
+    *standard in*, one per line; blank lines are ignored.
+
+Unlike :command:`pysemver sort`, the result is printed on *standard out*
+as JSON, so it can be parsed with ``fromJSON()`` or ``jq``: a JSON string
+holding the version, or ``null`` if there is no non-prerelease version
+(including when the input is empty)::
+
+    $ pysemver latest 1.10.0 1.2.0 2.0.0-rc.1
+    "1.10.0"
+
+    $ pysemver latest 2.0.0-rc.1
+    null
+
+If any argument (or input line) is not a valid version, nothing is printed on standard out
+and the command exits with return code 2.
+
+
